    "b-sks-rc-history-intro": {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "HISTORY — A target has a lifecycle, and its history is the ledger of what was done to it: mostly attempts, plus the things an attempt cannot tell on its own. One "
        },
        {
          "insert": "history_entry",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " row per event, keyed to the target by "
        },
        {
          "insert": "target_id",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", append-only, ordered by "
        },
        {
          "insert": "at",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ". Each row says what happened ("
        },
        {
          "insert": "kind",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ") and how it ended ("
        },
        {
          "insert": "outcome",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "); the detail lives in the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "attempt",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " row that "
        },
        {
          "insert": "attempt_id",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " points at, and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "history_ref",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " rows name the attempt, epoch, PR, or commit involved. The row that matters most is "
        },
        {
          "insert": "invalidated",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ": a later worker picking the target up must see that a previous attempt reached a match and was then invalidated at the epoch boundary (merge conflict, upstream change), so it knows the code worked and has to be blended into the current tree rather than re-derived. Run telemetry (queue, retry, claim, heartbeat, epoch open and close) stays in run state; it is not part of the knowledge system and never becomes a row here."
        }
      ],
      "id": "b-sks-rc-history-intro",
      "children": []
    },
